      includes ".encryption-verify" (storage.go:24), and "." sorts before "a",
      so the verify file is decrypted successfully first. Instrumented copy
      (log.Printf of decryptedSoFar at the failure site):
        $ go test -count=1 -v -run TestDisableEncryptionPartialFailureNothingDecryptedYet ./internal/services/storage/
          INSTR filesToDecrypt=[.../001/.encryption-verify .../001/aaa-broken.csv]
          INSTR failing on .../001/aaa-broken.csv,
                decryptedSoFar=[.../001/.encryption-verify] (len=1)
          --- PASS
      len(decryptedSoFar)==1, not 0. The test passes because it asserts only
      that the file is named and that IsEncrypted stays true -- both of which
      hold on the OTHER branch. It never asserts the absence of a re-encryption
      claim, which is the entire content of point 3.
  WHAT IS MISSING, precisely: (i) the fixture must make the failing file sort
  before ".encryption-verify" (e.g. name it ".a-broken.csv") so decryptedSoFar
  is genuinely empty, and (ii) the test must assert
  !strings.Contains(err.Error(), "re-encrypted"). With those two changes the
  mut3 mutation is detected; without them the guard can be deleted and nothing
  in the repo notices.

