BEST-EFFORT vs STOP-AT-FIRST-FAILURE -- independent read: best-effort is right.
  Each file is an independent exposure; no ordering or consistency invariant
  couples them, so aborting after the first restore failure converts a partial
  exposure into a strictly larger one with nothing gained. rollback is also
  monotone: for any given file it either re-encrypts it or leaves it exactly as
  it was, so continuing cannot make any file worse. The usual argument for
  fail-fast -- stop before you compound the damage -- has no purchase here. And
  the information fail-fast would have surfaced is not lost, because the helper
  returns the complete list rather than the first failure. Both arms chose this
  and both were right, for the reason the code comment gives.

